module

public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.TensorProduct.RightExactness
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Flat.Tensor
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Cotangent
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Flat.TorCriterion
import StacksCore.Tactic.EgaSgaAttribute

/-!
# Flatness of a first-order thickening and the conormal map

Let `A'` be a commutative ring, `I` an ideal of `A'` with `I ^ 2 = 0`, and write `A = A' ⧸ I`.
Let `B'` be a commutative `A'`-algebra and `B` a quotient of `B'` which is at the same time an
`A`-algebra compatibly, so that the square of ring maps `A' → B'`, `A' → A`, `B' → B`, `A → B`
commutes; the kernel `J` of `B' → B` then automatically contains the image of `I`.  This is the
affine picture of a morphism of first-order thickenings `(X ⊂ X') → (S ⊂ S')`, with
`S' = Spec A'`, `S = Spec A`, `X' = Spec B'` and `X = Spec B`.

The conormal module of `S ⊂ S'` is `I`, viewed as a module over `A` because `I ^ 2 = 0`, and its
pullback to `X` is `I ⊗[A] B`, which is `I ⊗[A'] B'` as soon as `J` is generated by the image of
`I`.  The canonical map to the conormal module `J` of `X ⊂ X'` multiplies inside `B'`: it carries
`i ⊗ b` to `i • b`.  Assuming `A → B` flat, this file proves that the canonical map is an
isomorphism exactly when `A' → B'` is flat and `J` is generated by the image of `I`, which is the
commutative algebra heart of Stacks 063Y.

Both directions run through the Tor criterion for flatness.  If `A' → B'` is flat then
`I ⊗[A'] B' → B'` is injective, directly from the characterisation of flatness by injectivity of
`I ⊗ - → -` on ideals, and its image is `I B' = J`, so it is an isomorphism onto `J`.
Conversely injectivity of `I ⊗[A'] B' → B'` makes `Tor_1^{A'}(A ⧸ I, B')` vanish, surjectivity
onto `J` identifies `B ⧸ I B'` with `B`, which is flat over `A`, and Lemma 051C upgrades these two
facts to flatness of `B'` over `A'` because `I ^ 2 = 0`.

## Main definitions

* `Ideal.cotangentEquivOfSqZero`: for a square-zero ideal the cotangent module `I ⧸ I ^ 2` is `I`.
* `Ideal.tensorSMulHom`: the canonical map `I ⊗[A'] B' → B'`, `i ⊗ b ↦ i • b`.
* `Ideal.tensorSMulHomToKer`: the same map with codomain the kernel of `B' → B`.
* `Ideal.tensorEquivTensorQuotSMulTop`: the pullback of the conormal module of `S ⊂ S'` is
  computed on the fibre product `S ×_{S'} X'`, that is `I ⊗[A'] B' ≃ I ⊗[A'] (B' ⧸ I B')`.
* `Ideal.tensorEquivTensorQuot`: when `J = I B'` the source of the canonical map is `I ⊗[A'] B`.
* `Ideal.quotSMulTopEquivOfKer`: when `J = I B'` the ring `B` is the fibre product `B' ⧸ I B'`.
* `Ideal.tensorFibreEquiv`: the pullback `I ⊗[A'] B` of the conormal module of `S ⊂ S'` to `X`,
  presented as the quotient of `I ⊗[A'] B'` by the image of `I ⊗[A'] J`.
* `Ideal.fibreTensorSMulHom`: the canonical map `I ⊗[A'] B → B'` out of that pullback,
  `i ⊗ b ↦ i • b'` for any lift `b'` of `b`, well defined because `J ^ 2 = 0`.
* `Ideal.fibreTensorSMulHomToKer`: the same map with codomain the kernel of `B' → B`.

## Main results

* `Ideal.bijective_tensorSMulHomToKer_iff`: Stacks 063Y in the affine case.  With `A → B` flat and
  `I ^ 2 = 0`, the canonical map `I ⊗[A'] B' → J` is an isomorphism if and only if `A' → B'` is
  flat and `J = I B'`.
* `Ideal.range_tensorSMulHom`: the image of the canonical map is `I B'`, with no hypotheses.
* `Ideal.injective_tensorSMulHom_iff`: injectivity of the canonical map is injectivity of
  `I ⊗[A'] B' → A' ⊗[A'] B'`, which is what the flatness criteria speak about.
* `Ideal.smul_top_eq_bot_of_pow_two_eq_bot`: a square-zero ideal annihilates itself.
* `Ideal.bijective_fibreTensorSMulHomToKer_iff`: the same equivalence as
  `Ideal.bijective_tensorSMulHomToKer_iff` for the canonical map `I ⊗[A'] B → J` out of the
  pullback of the conormal module, which is the module condition (2) of the source speaks about.
  Unlike the version with source `I ⊗[A'] B'`, this one uses the square-zero condition on `J`.
* `Ideal.smul_eq_zero_of_mem_ker`: a square-zero `J` is annihilated by `I`, which is what makes
  the canonical map out of `I ⊗[A'] B` well defined.
* `Ideal.fibreTensorSMulHom_comp_lTensor`: the canonical map out of `I ⊗[A'] B` precomposed with
  the surjection from `I ⊗[A'] B'` is `Ideal.tensorSMulHom`, which pins it down.
* `Ideal.bijective_lTensor_algebraLinearMap`: when `J = I B'` that surjection is bijective, so
  the two forms of the canonical map have the same bijectivity.

## Implementation notes

The source of the canonical map is taken to be `I ⊗[A'] B'` rather than `I ⊗[A] B`.  The two
agree once `J = I B'` — which is `Ideal.tensorEquivTensorQuot` together with the identification
of `I` with the cotangent module `I ⧸ I ^ 2` — and `I ⊗[A'] B'` is the form in which the
flatness criteria apply: injectivity of the canonical map is literally injectivity of
`I ⊗[A'] B' → A' ⊗[A'] B'`, which is what both the characterisation of flatness by ideals and
the vanishing of `Tor_1^{A'}(A', B')` speak about.  Writing the source as a tensor product over
`A` would in addition force the conormal module to be spelled as `Ideal.Cotangent`, since `I`
itself carries no `A`-module structure by instance search.

The square-zero condition on `J` is part of the geometric setting and is not used for the form of
the statement whose source is `I ⊗[A'] B'`: there the statement and the proof are the same for an
arbitrary ideal `J`.  It *is* used for the form whose source is the pullback `I ⊗[A'] B` of the
conormal module, `Ideal.bijective_fibreTensorSMulHomToKer_iff`, because `J ^ 2 = 0` is exactly
what makes `i ⊗ b ↦ i • b'` independent of the lift `b'` of `b`
(`Ideal.smul_eq_zero_of_mem_ker`).  The containment of the image of `I` in `J` is in neither form
a hypothesis, being a consequence of the commuting square
(`Ideal.smul_top_le_ker_algebraMap`).

That an elementary tensor `i ⊗ y` with `y ∈ I B'` vanishes in `I ⊗[A'] B'` is already in the
library twice, as `Ideal.tmul_eq_zero_of_mem_smul_top` of
`StacksFrontier.RingTheory.GradedAlgebra.LiftPresentation` and privately in
`StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Flat.SquareZeroBaseChange`; it is repeated here privately rather than
importing a graded-algebra file into a flatness file.

